From c66e78447b2dc769f06bd82de449b989fba5d719 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Matt Harris <mharris@vultr.com>
Date: Mon, 18 May 2026 20:40:14 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] feat(quark): route MXFP4 MoE through AITER Triton kernels
 on gfx942

AITER's CK MXFP4 GEMM kernels are compile-time excluded on gfx942
(dispatch.h enables them only on gfx95). On gfx942 the AITER MoeRunner
path raises at inference time; the Triton kernels in
aiter/ops/triton/moe/moe_op_mxfp4*.py work because they use
tl.dot_scaled, which decomposes FP4 ops into BF16 upcast + BF16 MFMA.

Route QuarkW4A4MXFp4MoE.apply_weights to a dedicated Triton dispatch
(triton_mxfp4_dispatch.triton_mxfp4_moe) on gfx942. The dispatch is
CUDA-graph-capture safe: all GPU tensors are pre-allocated in a
workspace cache on the first call and reused on subsequent calls.

Workspace caching strategy: keyed on a bucketed M value rather than
exact M. Without bucketing, every unique runtime M (which varies per
step under DP attention) allocates a new ~hundreds-of-MB entry that
is never freed; in a representative Kimi-K2.6-MXFP4 run the cache grew
to 98 entries / ~50 GiB per DP in ~13 minutes and crashed every TP
worker with HIP out-of-memory. The fixed bucket schedule caps the
cache at O(len(_M_BUCKETS)) entries per (top_k, num_experts,
hidden_dim, inter_dim, device) tuple while still hitting on every
graph-replay batch size.

Concurrent prefill under DP attention drives the post-allgather M
above the largest workspace bucket; for top_k=8 routed-expert MoEs
this would require ~10 GiB allocations that hipMalloc cannot satisfy
on top of in-flight activations, hanging the GPU. Chunk M to
_MAX_M_PER_CALL=16384 inside triton_mxfp4_moe so every per-call
workspace fits in the bucket schedule; per-chunk overhead is
dominated by kernel-launch latency and is negligible relative to the
GEMM cost.

Diagnostic counters (calls/hits/misses + per-miss MB summary) are
kept in the dispatch so cache growth can be correlated with HBM use
in future investigations.

Not upstreamed.
